1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 In this demo, we'll be creating a program that utilizes ranges.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
 Once you're ready, fire up Visual Studio Code, and let's go.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
 Once Visual Studio Code loads navigate to the demo folder.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 We are on the ranges demo, so click on the ranges folder,

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 and then double click on ranges.go.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
 So when we use the range keyword, it's going to automatically

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
 create an iterator for us.

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
 And so it's kind of like an easier way of making a for loop with the counter.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 So let's start by making a slice.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 So like a string slice.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 We'll have a low world and an exclamation point.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 So we'll have four I comma element.

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
 Sign equals range slice.

14
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 So when we use range, we're going to get two things back from this slice.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 I will be the index number.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 And element will be the thing in the slice.

17
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 So it's always going to start at zero for I.

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 And then elements in this case will be hello.

19
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 And then the second iteration will be world.

20
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 And then the third will be the exclamation point.

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 So we'll have a we'll do a print line that we can see what gets printed out.

22
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 So we'll print I and elements.

23
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
 Let's just run that as is and then we'll add some more to it.

24
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 So I'll hop into a terminal.

25
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 You can run this with go run dot slash demo slash ranges.

26
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 And so here we have the zero hello one world to exclamation point.

27
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 So now we that we have the range keyword.

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 We're going to be able to iterate over individual letters of these strings.

29
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
 If you'll recall from our previous lecture,

30
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 I did mention that we're not able to go through letters by using a typical for loop.

31
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
 So if we had a loop like this and we hit something like that.

32
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 And try to go into the slice.

33
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 Like that to access the letters of hello.

34
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:08,000
 This isn't going to work because we're going through individual bytes of that string.

35
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
 And if you'll recall, the letters take up more than one byte in some cases,

36
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
 like special characters or emoji for example.

37
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 So we're not able to do this to access each letter.

38
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 However, when we use the range keyword,

39
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 it's going to automatically account for all that for us.

40
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 And so we are able to go through each letter when we have the range keyword.

41
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 So let me delete this.

42
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
 And now we'll go through each letter.

43
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 So I'm going to ignore the index for this case.

44
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
 And I'll just use CH and CH will just be short for our character.

45
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
 I'll go from a range of elements.

46
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
 So this outer loop here is going to go through each one of these words.

47
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 And this inner loop is going to go through each letter of each word.

48
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
 I'll do a print F in this case.

49
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 A little bit of spaces.

50
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
 And if we do percent to Q, we'll actually print out the glyph representation of these runes.

51
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
 Because each one of these is a rune.

52
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 I'm also going to add a little bit here.

53
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
 So now thanks to range, we get the length.

54
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 The first range here, we're getting the length of the string automatically for us

55
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
 because we don't have to check to see how far long we are in the iteration.

56
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 And on the second range, we're getting the same thing.

57
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
 We're getting the length of the total number of characters in these strings, not the bytes.

58
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 So we're going to get individual letters regardless of how many bytes they consume

59
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,000
 and how complex they are.

60
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
 And when we use percent Q within this print F function,

61
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
 we're able to print out a rune instead of just the number of the rune.

62
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
 So let's hop back into a terminal and see what happens.

63
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 And so on our first iteration, we print out hello.

64
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
 And then each iteration of the inner loop, we have each letter being printed out.

65
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 And the same thing for world and the same thing for the exclamation.

66
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
 This is just a short demo on the range keyword.

67
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 It's pretty straightforward.

68
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
 And for the most part, simulates the typical for loop with the iteration and the counter.

69
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:19,000
 Except it also takes into account the glyph representation of these string and runes.

70
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:26,000
 We're also able to use a range on different data types that will be covering later on in the course.

71
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 And finally, one of the most convenient thing about ranges is we don't have to calculate the length or anything,

72
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,000
 because all of that is considered within the range keyword itself.

73
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:41,000
 And it's totally normal to ignore either the index or the element depending on what you're trying to do.

74
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
 Let's conclude the demo on the range keyword.

75
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
 I'll see you in the next presentation.

